  "text": "The line segment $P_1P_2$ is perpendicular to the line passing through the centers of the circles $C_1$ and $C_2$. The centers of the circles $C_1$ and $C_2$ are $(1, 2)$ and $(-1, 3)$, respectively. The slope of the line passing through these two points is $\\frac{3-2}{-1-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}$. Therefore, the slope of the line $P_1P_2$ is the negative reciprocal of this slope, which is $2$.\n\nNow, we work inwards. The center of circle $C_1$, $(1, 2)$ is at a distance of $1$ unit from the line $P_1P_2$, and the center of circle $C_2$, $(-1, 3)$ is at a distance of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ units from the line $P_1P_2$. The equation of the line $P_1P_2$ in point-slope form can be written as:\n\n$y-2=mx+b$\n\n$m=2$\n\n$b=-Ax+A$, where $A$ is the x-coordinate of the point $(1, 2)$ on the line segment $P_1P_2$. Substituting $x=1$ and $y=2$ gives:\n\n$2-2=2(1)+b$\n\n$b=-2$ \n\nTherefore, the equation of the line $P_1P_2$ in point-slope form becomes:\n\n$y-2=2x-2$\n\n$y=2x$",
